item.text:
Searches covered the UN experts’ underlying reports and annexes, FARDC proxy cooperation and corrective orders, Luanda/Nairobi implementation, P-DDRCS implementation, military justice, and audited 2022 public accounts. The strongest upgrade is the split extraction of S/2022/967: it establishes senior-FARDC-supported proxy cooperation while separately recording Tshisekedi’s public prohibition, command reshuffle, and an initially observed withdrawal from front lines. Later UN reporting shows that cooperation nevertheless persisted, so presidential correction was incomplete. A verified Kibumba handover supplies limited ceasefire implementation evidence, but no general ceasefire, prisoner-release, humanitarian-access, or completed DDR implementation was verified. The Court of Accounts report confirms serious aggregate accounting and payroll-control deficiencies but does not isolate defence procurement, payroll, or off-budget expenditure; 2B.8 therefore remains substantially unresolved. I found no reliable operation-specific record tying Tshisekedi to authorization of civilian harm, nor a presidential investigation or remedy addressing FARDC violations or state-of-siege abuses.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Midterm report of the Group of Experts on the Democratic Republic of the Congo","publisher":"United Nations Security Council","publication_date":"2022-12-16","url":"https://documents.un.org/api/symbol/access?l=en&s=s%2F2022%2F967&t=pdf","claim":"The Group found that CMC/FDP, APCLS, FDLR, NDC-R and MPA combatants fought against M23/RDF, sometimes jointly with FARDC, in May-June 2022; coalition members subsequently received weapons and ammunition from FARDC members on several occasions. The finding attributes organization and material cooperation to FARDC personnel, not to Tshisekedi personally.","locator":"S/2022/967, paras. 64-69, especially paras. 68-69; annexes 41 and 46-48; source bases identified in footnotes 47-49","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C1-001","canonical_fact_key":"fardc_armed_group_cooperation_2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.5","2B.9","2B.10"],"source_type":"UN sanctions-monitoring expert report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UN expert finding based on combatants, local authorities, civil society, researchers, intelligence sources, documentary material, and photographic/video evidence, with precise paragraph and annex locators.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct contemporaneous findings concerning May-June 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Institutional and command-responsibility context only: cooperation is attributed to FARDC members and officers; the report does not establish presidential authorization or knowledge before the conduct.","contrary_evidence":"The same report records Tshisekedi’s express condemnation of proxies and subsequent command changes.","underlying_fact_key":"fardc_armed_group_cooperation_2022","lenses":["2B.5","2B.9","2B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Midterm report of the Group of Experts on the Democratic Republic of the Congo — presidential prohibition and command response","publisher":"United Nations Security Council","publication_date":"2022-12-16","url":"https://documents.un.org/api/symbol/access?l=en&s=s%2F2022%2F967&t=pdf","claim":"On 12 May 2022 Tshisekedi explicitly denounced FARDC use of armed-group proxies. After persistent allegations, Brigadier-General Peter Cirimwami was reassigned on 6 July and the North Kivu command was reshuffled; newly appointed authorities instructed armed groups to leave the front lines, which the groups mostly did at that stage.","locator":"S/2022/967, para. 70 and annex 50, pp. 176-177 of the UN PDF; annex 50 identifies the 12 May military-deontology seminar, Cirimwami’s 6 July reassignment, the July command reshuffle, and the reported withdrawal instruction","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C1-002","canonical_fact_key":"tshisekedi_proxy_prohibition_command_correction_2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.5","2B.9"],"source_type":"UN sanctions-monitoring expert report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UN expert report provides an attributable presidential statement, dated personnel measures, and reported initial implementation, while preserving the distinction between prohibition and later compliance.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"All identified decisions and initial effects occurred during May-July 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct for the public prohibition; proportional strategic attribution for the command reshuffle. The report does not establish that Tshisekedi personally ordered every personnel action.","contrary_evidence":"FARDC cooperation had already occurred, and the Group’s subsequent report found that senior-officer-supported cooperation continued despite the presidential instruction.","underlying_fact_key":"tshisekedi_proxy_prohibition_command_correction_2022","lenses":["2B.5","2B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Final report of the Group of Experts on the Democratic Republic of the Congo","publisher":"United Nations Security Council","publication_date":"2023-06-13","url":"https://documents.un.org/api/symbol/access?l=en&s=s%2F2023%2F431&t=pdf","claim":"Reviewing conduct extending from 2022, the Group stated that despite Tshisekedi’s May 2022 instruction to refrain from proxies, a coalition of armed groups continued fighting alongside FARDC and that its engagement was organized, coordinated, and supported by senior FARDC officers. This establishes incomplete correction, but not presidential authorization of the continued cooperation.","locator":"S/2023/431, paras. 71-72, under “Armed group coalitions and coordination by the Armed Forces of the Democratic Republic of the Congo”; para. 71 expressly cross-references S/2022/967, paras. 64-71","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C1-003","canonical_fact_key":"continued_fardc_proxy_cooperation_despite_order_2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.5","2B.9"],"source_type":"UN sanctions-monitoring expert report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Authoritative later UN finding specifically evaluates the effectiveness of a dated 2022 presidential instruction and identifies the responsible organizational level as senior FARDC officers.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Later publication used only for its specific finding about the May 2022 instruction and continuation of cooperation originating in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Supports ineffective prevention or correction under presidential command authority, not personal direction, approval, or demonstrated contemporaneous knowledge of each continued incident.","contrary_evidence":"Tshisekedi expressly prohibited proxy use and the July 2022 reshuffle initially induced many armed groups to withdraw from front lines.","underlying_fact_key":"continued_fardc_proxy_cooperation_despite_order_2022","lenses":["2B.5","2B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Midterm report of the Group of Experts on the Democratic Republic of the Congo — RDF intervention and support to M23","publisher":"United Nations Security Council","publication_date":"2022-12-16","url":"https://documents.un.org/api/symbol/access?l=en&s=s%2F2022%2F967&t=pdf","claim":"The Group found RDF military operations inside DRC, conducted unilaterally and jointly with M23, and RDF troop reinforcement of M23 for operations including the May attack on Rumangabo and the June seizure of Bunagana. Rwanda denied crossing the border or supporting M23. This is a distinct external driver of the adverse 2022 security trend.","locator":"S/2022/967, paras. 47-51, especially paras. 49-51; annexes 16 and 26-33","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C1-004","canonical_fact_key":"rdf_m23_external_intervention_2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.10"],"source_type":"UN sanctions-monitoring expert report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UN expert finding supported by eyewitnesses, imagery, documentary evidence, combatant testimony, and military and intelligence sources; the report also records Rwanda’s denial.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct evidence of operations during May-October 2022 and published before year-end.","ruler_attribution":"External/contextual driver; it should not be treated as conduct controlled by Tshisekedi, though his government’s military and diplomatic response remains evaluable separately.","contrary_evidence":"Rwanda denied intervention and characterized the allegations as scapegoating for DRC internal failures.","underlying_fact_key":"rdf_m23_external_intervention_2022","lenses":["2B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"M23 to withdraw from Rumangabo and Kishishe","publisher":"Ministry of Defence, Kenya / East African Community Regional Force","publication_date":"2022-12-30","url":"https://www.mod.go.ke/news/m23-to-withdraw-from-rumagambo-and-kishishe/","claim":"EACRF, the Ad Hoc Verification Mechanism, and the Expanded Joint Verification Mechanism acknowledged M23’s withdrawal from Kibumba and EACRF’s takeover as a verified but partial Luanda milestone; they then sought further withdrawals from Rumangabo and Kishishe. The record supports limited implementation, not a general ceasefire or complete territorial withdrawal.","locator":"Paragraphs beginning “A delegation from the East African Community Regional Force” and “The delegation acknowledged the withdrawal”; account of the 29 December briefing to Tshisekedi","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C1-005","canonical_fact_key":"luanda_partial_kibumba_implementation_2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.7","2B.10"],"source_type":"official regional-force verification statement","source_confidence":"medium-high","source_confidence_reason":"Official operational statement involving three verification bodies and a specified location, although it is not an independent humanitarian or UN assessment and documents only a narrow milestone.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The withdrawal, takeover, and presidential briefing occurred on 23-29 December 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Tshisekedi participated in and received reporting on the regional deployment; the physical withdrawal was implemented by M23 and EACRF, not personally by him.","contrary_evidence":"The source itself describes a continuing sequence and calls for additional withdrawals; it does not establish a complete ceasefire, humanitarian access, prisoner release, or DDR.","underlying_fact_key":"luanda_partial_kibumba_implementation_2022","lenses":["2B.7","2B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"National P-DDRCS strategy adoption workshop","publisher":"Programme de Désarmement, Démobilisation, Relèvement Communautaire et Stabilisation","publication_date":"2022-03-28","url":"https://pddrcs.cd/kinshasa-adoption-de-la-strategie-nationale-du-programme-de-desarmement-demobilisation-relevement-communautaire-et-stabilisation-p-ddrcs/","claim":"The government convened and opened the March 2022 workshop adopting the national P-DDRCS strategy, addressing disarmament, demobilization, community recovery, stabilization, armed-group financing, security governance, and foreign armed groups. This demonstrates institutional program formation, but the record supplies no independently verified outputs such as weapons collected, combatants demobilized, or communities stabilized during 2022.","locator":"Opening paragraphs identifying the 28 March 2022 workshop and the section describing the strategy’s stated scope and merger of PNDDR and STAREC","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C1-006","canonical_fact_key":"pddrcs_strategy_adoption_2022","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.7"],"source_type":"official programme statement","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Primary evidence of a government program decision and its stated scope, but self-reported and lacking independently measured implementation results.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly concerns a March 2022 government action.","ruler_attribution":"The strategy was government policy associated with Tshisekedi’s stabilization agenda, but this source does not establish his personal management or successful implementation.","contrary_evidence":"Absence of quantified or independently verified 2022 implementation means adoption cannot be treated as completed DDR performance.","underlying_fact_key":"pddrcs_strategy_adoption_2022","lenses":["2B.7"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Report on control of execution of the central-government budget for fiscal year 2022","publisher":"Cour des comptes de la République démocratique du Congo","publication_date":"2023-12-05","url":"https://courdescomptes.cd/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/RAPPORT-POUVOIR-CENTRAL-2022.pdf","claim":"The Court found aggregate central-government personnel-credit overruns of CDF 927.46 billion for institutions and CDF 1,120.17 billion for ministries, while the government failed to provide the requested explanatory report; it also found CDF 129.88 billion in unused remuneration credits and CDF 291.88 billion in wholly unexecuted personnel credits. These are audited control failures but are not disaggregated sufficiently to establish defence patronage, procurement abuse, ghost soldiers, or presidential beneficiaries.","locator":"Report pp. 48-50, section “Dépassement sur crédits limitatifs” and subsections on under-consumption and non-consumption of personnel credits; especially printed p. 49; LOFIP arts. 38 and 51 cited by the Court","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C1-007","canonical_fact_key":"drc_2022_aggregate_payroll_control_failures","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.8"],"source_type":"supreme-audit-institution report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official ex-post Court of Accounts review with exact amounts and legal standards, but the cited tables aggregate institutions and ministries rather than isolating defence transactions or patronage networks.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Later audit directly examines fiscal-year 2022 execution.","ruler_attribution":"National fiscal-control context only; no identified expenditure, beneficiary, authorization, or omission is directly attributed to Tshisekedi.","contrary_evidence":"The report’s aggregate accounting defects prevent treating the 2022 defence spending total as a clean measure of military capability or patronage; they do not themselves prove patronage.","underlying_fact_key":"drc_2022_aggregate_payroll_control_failures","lenses":["2B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Ituri military trial concerning sale of ammunition to CODECO","publisher":"Agence Congolaise de Presse","publication_date":"2022-05-12","url":"https://acp.cd/nation/ituri-affaire-vente-des-munitions-a-la-milice-codeco-la-peine-de-mort-requise-contre-trois-officiers-superieurs-des-fardc/","claim":"Military prosecutors publicly pursued three senior FARDC officers and other defendants for allegedly selling military ammunition to CODECO; this is evidence that competent authorities took a concrete judicial step against military supply to an armed group, although the prosecution allegation at this stage was not yet a final finding and was not shown to have been initiated by Tshisekedi.","locator":"Dateline Bunia, 12 May 2022; opening paragraph reporting the prosecution’s final submissions before the Ituri Military Court in the ammunition-sale case","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C1-008","canonical_fact_key":"ituri_codeco_ammunition_prosecution_2022","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.5","2B.9"],"source_type":"official state news report of judicial proceeding","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Specific contemporaneous report of a public military-court proceeding, but it reports prosecutorial allegations rather than a final adjudicated finding and gives no presidential chain of instruction.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"The prosecution occurred in May 2022 and concerned contemporary FARDC-armed-group supply allegations.","ruler_attribution":"Competent-authority corrective action only; no evidence of presidential initiation, knowledge, obstruction, or ownership.","contrary_evidence":"The proceeding shows some enforcement capacity, but does not remedy or explain the separately documented operational cooperation between FARDC officers, FDLR, and other anti-M23 groups.","underlying_fact_key":"ituri_codeco_ammunition_prosecution_2022","lenses":["2B.5","2B.9"]}
